      "id": 1,
      "text": "Morrowville was founded in 1884 and named after its founder, Cal Morrow, a state senator, landowner, and cattleman.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://washingtoncountyks.gov/our-county/morrowville/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Morrowville was laid out in 1884 when the Missouri and Burlington Railroad was set to reach the town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morrowville%2C_Kansas"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1923, farmer James D. Cummings and draftsman John Earl McLeod designed the first bulldozer in Morrowville.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morrowville%2C_Kansas"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A replica of that original bulldozer now sits in the city park, honoring the town’s engineering heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morrowville%2C_Kansas"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In 1967, Morrowville and Haddam schools merged into USD #221, forming the current local school district.",
